                     FIRST CALL FOR VOTES (of 2)
               unmoderated group comp.os.linux.powerpc

Newsgroups line:
comp.os.linux.powerpc	Linux systems running on PowerPC microprocessors.

Votes must be received by 23:59:59 UTC, 4 Sep 1997.

This vote is being conducted by a neutral third party.  Questions
about the proposed group should be directed to the proponents.

Proponent: Christopher Penney <penney@cps.msu.edu>
Proponent: Shimpei Yamashita <shimpei@socrates.caltech.edu>

Votetaker: Rebecca McQuitty <mcq@wco.com>

RATIONALE: comp.os.linux.powerpc

Ports of Linux to the PowerPC microprocessor have been in existence
since 1995. In the past year and a half, however, Linux on PowerPC
gained considerable momentum, with three different kernel flavors
existing concurrently: the original Linux/PPC port by Gary Thomas
running on motherboards from Motorola, Be, and IBM
(http://www.linuxppc.org/); the Mach microkernel-hosted "MkLinux" port
sponsored by Apple Computer and OSF that runs on some Power
Macintoshes and clones (http://www.mklinux.apple.com/); and a
monolithic Linux kernel port by Paul Mackerras
(http://www.cs.wisc.edu/~tesch/linux_info/). A group at New Mexico
Tech (http://www.cs.nmt.edu/~linuxppc/) is working on integrating the
PReP and the PowerMac ports into a single source tree.

The discussions on these projects currently take place in mailing
lists dedicated to each of the ports. In recent months, however, the
mailing lists have had overwhelming traffic (the MkLinux lists
combined had 1190 messages for June 1997, and the Linux-pmac list had
771), making the mailing lists impractical for many people. In
addition, the current setup does not allow users of different flavors
of PowerPC Linux to interact with each other easily, when they have
much to gain from each other (all three flavors are binary compatible,
and the userland software are mostly identical). We feel that a
newsgroup will solve both of these problems.

The newsgroup is not aimed to supplant any of the existing mailing
lists, but to supplement and extend them. We believe that the
newsgroup will be a more accessible venue of discussion for many users
who cannot follow such a heavy traffic of mail, while the the quality
of the lists themselves will improve as the traffic level winds down
to a more manageable level.

CHARTER: comp.os.linux.powerpc

Comp.os.linux.powerpc is for discussion about Linux running on
systems based on the PowerPC microprocessor family.

The posting of commercial information to this group is permitted
only if the information is directly relevant to Linux on PowerPC.
Spamming of any sort is absolutely not tolerated.

END CHARTER.

IMPORTANT VOTING INFORMATION:

Standard Guidelines for voting apply.  One vote per person, one vote
per account.  Votes must be mailed directly from the voter to the
votetaker.  Anonymous, forwarded or proxy votes are not valid.  Votes
mailed by WWW/HTML/CGI forms are considered anonymous votes.

Addresses and votes of all voters will be published in the final
voting results post.  Duplicate votes will be resolved in favor of the
most recent valid vote.

The purpose of a Usenet vote is to determine the genuine interest of
persons who would read a proposed newsgroup.  Soliciting votes from
disinterested parties defeats this purpose.  Please do *not*
distribute this CFV; instead, direct people to the official CFV posted
to news.announce.newgroups.  Distributing pre-marked, incomplete, or
otherwise edited copies of this CFV is generally considered to be vote
fraud.

When in doubt, ask the votetaker.

HOW TO VOTE:

Extract the ballot from the CFV by deleting everything before the
